00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:44.975
- How much time does it
take to have a good idea?

00:00:44.975 --> 00:00:45.808
Zero.

00:00:46.759 --> 00:00:48.602
How much time does it take to be creative?

00:00:48.602 --> 00:00:49.435
Zero.

00:00:50.312 --> 00:00:52.275
How much time does it take
to be loving and present

00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:54.271
with your kids when you
tuck 'em in bed at night?

00:00:54.271 --> 00:00:58.294
Zero, those do not require
time, they require space.

00:00:58.294 --> 00:00:59.995
If you're wrapped up around a problem,

00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:02.973
an issue, you know, backlog
of all kinds of things

00:01:02.973 --> 00:01:05.753
unhandled and
inappropriately engaged with,

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:08.190
very difficult to have creative ideas,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.024
be innovative, or be loving and present.

00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:13.191
You need room.

00:01:17.173 --> 00:01:20.000
Oh, come on, Leonardo
da Vinci had 24 hours.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.047
Mother Teresa had 24 hours.

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:25.183
Johann Sebastian Bach had 24 hours.

00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:27.360
You know, I know he didn't have WhatsApp,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.277
but he had 20 kids, take your pick.

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:36.018
You need room.

00:01:36.018 --> 00:01:38.768
(pleasant music)

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.134
- Here's how I want you to work through

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.911
your day from now on.

00:01:42.911 --> 00:01:45.791
First, when you wake up, your goal

00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:49.373
is not to go to the computer
and check your inbox.

00:01:49.373 --> 00:01:51.387
We've got to tear you away from the inbox,

00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:54.737
particularly if you
are a senior executive,

00:01:54.737 --> 00:01:57.702
an owner of a business,
a person who has major

00:01:57.702 --> 00:02:00.153
projects that you're moving forward.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.022
If you are a customer
service person and your

00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:05.136
job is to check email all day, then hey,

00:02:05.136 --> 00:02:08.341
I get it, that's okay, but for most folks,

00:02:08.341 --> 00:02:11.041
their real goal is not
to check email all day,

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:13.506
it's to be productive and get things done,

00:02:13.506 --> 00:02:15.666
so unless it's your job
to be on the email drop,

00:02:15.666 --> 00:02:18.689
where you respond to everything
within 10 or 15 minutes,

00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:20.725
then this is really about
taking ownership of your

00:02:20.725 --> 00:02:23.459
life again, and I think
those folks who are in that

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:26.499
position will also find
this to be very inspiring,

00:02:26.499 --> 00:02:29.699
and a wise guide to
working more effectively.

00:02:29.699 --> 00:02:31.232
So, let's get to it.

00:02:31.232 --> 00:02:32.473
Here's what I want you to do.

00:02:32.473 --> 00:02:34.022
When you wake up each
day, let's talk about

00:02:34.022 --> 00:02:35.063
productivity in general.

00:02:35.063 --> 00:02:37.563
When you wake up each day, the first thing

00:02:37.563 --> 00:02:39.886
to do is not go to the computer.

00:02:39.886 --> 00:02:43.252
The first thing to do is go exercise,

00:02:43.252 --> 00:02:46.922
go for a walk, take a
long shower, it's to open

00:02:46.922 --> 00:02:50.215
your body and engage, and
in doing that process,

00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:52.154
whatever it is for you,
some people it's a long run,

00:02:52.154 --> 00:02:53.681
some people it's a longer shower,

00:02:53.681 --> 00:02:55.570
some people it's a walk
around the neighborhood,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:56.663
when you're doing that,
here's what I want you

00:02:56.663 --> 00:02:58.915
to ask yourself, a
couple simple questions.

00:02:58.915 --> 00:03:02.858
First, I want you to ask
yourself what am I excited

00:03:02.858 --> 00:03:07.025
about today, or could I create
to be excited about today?

00:03:07.912 --> 00:03:09.047
So, what is it?

00:03:09.047 --> 00:03:11.620
I want you to begin your
day with enthusiasm.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.839
Optimism is the magical
formula that creates

00:03:14.839 --> 00:03:16.571
greater happiness in our lives.

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.331
To have something to look forward to.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:21.516
Steal away someone's
vision for a good future,

00:03:21.516 --> 00:03:24.475
and you quickly see them
fall into depression,

00:03:24.475 --> 00:03:26.937
but when we have a great
connection to something

00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:28.589
in the future that we look forward to,

00:03:28.589 --> 00:03:31.075
even if it's just this day, that creates

00:03:31.075 --> 00:03:33.667
the charge within us, so walk around,

00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:35.881
take your laps, do whatever you have to do

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:38.279
in the morning, but ask
yourself, what is it

00:03:38.279 --> 00:03:40.331
that I'm excited about today, or could be

00:03:40.331 --> 00:03:42.328
enthusiastic about today?

00:03:42.328 --> 00:03:46.860
Second, ask what am I
grateful for in life today?

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.759
You know, what am I so
appreciative of having?

00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:51.069
You know, I know a lot of
these videos I'm talking

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:53.353
so fast at you, but as
you get to know my work,

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:55.543
I'm a pretty soulful guy,
and I'm an incredibly

00:03:55.543 --> 00:03:58.537
grateful person for all that
I've been able to create,

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:00.656
all, you know, all the stages I get on,

00:04:00.656 --> 00:04:02.398
all the people who come to our seminars,

00:04:02.398 --> 00:04:04.080
the brands I've built, the opportunities

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.695
I've been blessed to have.

00:04:05.695 --> 00:04:08.055
Even the challenges I've
been blessed to have,

00:04:08.055 --> 00:04:10.287
and I think that, coming
from that place spiritually

00:04:10.287 --> 00:04:12.753
each day really opens you up to something

00:04:12.753 --> 00:04:16.047
magnificent in the
universe, maybe you agree.

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:18.515
And so, ask what can I
be enthusiastic about,

00:04:18.515 --> 00:04:22.402
what am I grateful for,
and then ask, third,

00:04:22.402 --> 00:04:25.350
what am I committed to
making happen today,

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.222
no matter what?

00:04:27.222 --> 00:04:29.363
If we start from those
positions, then we're in good,

00:04:29.363 --> 00:04:31.778
then we're in a good space,
then I want you to drink

00:04:31.778 --> 00:04:34.763
lots of water and have
a healthy breakfast.

00:04:34.763 --> 00:04:36.856
Lots of water, healthy breakfast.

00:04:36.856 --> 00:04:39.765
Now, we can start thinking about work.

00:04:39.765 --> 00:04:41.046
Deal?

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:43.489
This is the secret to
you performing better.

00:04:43.489 --> 00:04:46.237
We've got to get you in the
right mindset in the morning,

00:04:46.237 --> 00:04:48.027
we've got to take care
of your physical needs,

00:04:48.027 --> 00:04:52.576
of hydration and nourishment,
then walk into the office,

00:04:52.576 --> 00:04:54.111
then get ready to work.

00:04:54.111 --> 00:04:55.537
- Point number two, you
want to make sure you're

00:04:55.537 --> 00:04:57.776
producing the right things.

00:04:57.776 --> 00:05:00.871
You could make a ton of decisions,

00:05:00.871 --> 00:05:04.023
check off a lot of email,
make a ton of phone calls,

00:05:04.023 --> 00:05:05.911
and at the end of the
day, the end of the week,

00:05:05.911 --> 00:05:07.766
the end of the quarter,
the end of the year,

00:05:07.766 --> 00:05:10.428
not have accomplished
all that much at all.

00:05:10.428 --> 00:05:12.932
The way that I get around
that is looking at my

00:05:12.932 --> 00:05:15.897
list of goals, looking
at my list of priorities,

00:05:15.897 --> 00:05:18.652
looking at my list of
to-dos, and asking myself

00:05:18.652 --> 00:05:21.894
which of these, if done,
would make all of the

00:05:21.894 --> 00:05:24.448
other ones easier or unnecessary?

00:05:24.448 --> 00:05:27.363
This is a great way to narrow your focus

00:05:27.363 --> 00:05:29.542
to the most important domino that,

00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:33.930
when flipped over, will then
topple 20, 30, 40 others.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.638
One thing I keep in mind a lot,

00:05:35.638 --> 00:05:37.817
this is from Steve Jobs,
innovation is saying

00:05:37.817 --> 00:05:41.597
no to a thousand things, and really,

00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:43.613
if you want to do anything impressive,

00:05:43.613 --> 00:05:46.275
anything incredible, you have to say no

00:05:46.275 --> 00:05:49.025
to 99% of what comes at you.

00:05:50.254 --> 00:05:52.631
And that certainly applies to email,

00:05:52.631 --> 00:05:55.636
which is effectively everyone
else's agenda for your time.

00:05:55.636 --> 00:05:57.740
We all have the same 24 hours in the day,

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.447
and if you're like, "I don't have time,

00:05:59.447 --> 00:06:01.240
"I can't sleep, I need
to cut back on sleep,

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.043
"I need to do this, I need to do that,"

00:06:03.043 --> 00:06:05.922
if you don't have time,
you don't have priorities.

00:06:05.922 --> 00:06:06.885
That's it.

00:06:06.885 --> 00:06:09.318
- Your environment is so important,

00:06:09.318 --> 00:06:12.555
and if you look at B
performers or C producers,

00:06:12.555 --> 00:06:14.484
they really don't pay a lot of attention

00:06:14.484 --> 00:06:16.289
to their environment, and so they get very

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:18.708
distracted, there's lots of noises,

00:06:18.708 --> 00:06:20.695
their phones are going off, maybe there's

00:06:20.695 --> 00:06:23.019
news in the background, maybe there's

00:06:23.019 --> 00:06:25.819
messy environments all around them,

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:27.455
maybe there's toxic people.

00:06:27.455 --> 00:06:29.807
What I'm suggesting to you as the second

00:06:29.807 --> 00:06:33.183
piece of technology to really 100 times

00:06:33.183 --> 00:06:35.517
your productivity is this:

00:06:35.517 --> 00:06:39.684
Install environments of
tight bubbles of total focus.

00:06:41.355 --> 00:06:44.334
The way I run my work life on certain days

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:47.054
I am very hard to reach,
and I challenge you,

00:06:47.054 --> 00:06:49.711
with great love and respect, be very hard

00:06:49.711 --> 00:06:53.428
to reach during key times of your week.

00:06:53.428 --> 00:06:55.201
I mean, I see a lot of people,

00:06:55.201 --> 00:06:57.771
and it's not judging them,
it's just observing them,

00:06:57.771 --> 00:07:00.428
every time their phone
rings, they check it,

00:07:00.428 --> 00:07:02.921
or they have, you know,
these loud notifications,

00:07:02.921 --> 00:07:05.308
so they're constantly being distracted,

00:07:05.308 --> 00:07:06.757
and we know what the research says,

00:07:06.757 --> 00:07:10.924
the research says we spend 2.1
hours a day in distraction.

00:07:11.938 --> 00:07:15.735
We spe-, and it takes
21 minutes to refocus

00:07:15.735 --> 00:07:18.396
your focus after you've been distracted.

00:07:18.396 --> 00:07:21.169
So if you are being
distracted by notifications,

00:07:21.169 --> 00:07:23.754
checking your phone,
checking your Twitter feed,

00:07:23.754 --> 00:07:26.199
checking your Facebook
feed, looking on Instagram,

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:27.927
checking all the different platforms,

00:07:27.927 --> 00:07:29.891
that is going to destroy your focus.

00:07:29.891 --> 00:07:32.761
So the second piece of
technology is create these

00:07:32.761 --> 00:07:35.333
tight bubbles of total
focus, and the way I do it

00:07:35.333 --> 00:07:40.248
is I have certain days that
I label as creative days,

00:07:40.248 --> 00:07:44.242
and on those creative days,
I actually go device free,

00:07:44.242 --> 00:07:47.228
and on those days, I'm very hard to find,

00:07:47.228 --> 00:07:49.589
and on those days, I'm in total solitude,

00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:52.327
and on those days, I go to certain places,

00:07:52.327 --> 00:07:55.287
I call them my Menlo
Labs, just like Edison had

00:07:55.287 --> 00:07:58.751
his Menlo Lab, and these
places are very minimalist,

00:07:58.751 --> 00:08:00.938
and there's no one there, and that's where

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:03.926
I do my best work, so install these tight

00:08:03.926 --> 00:08:06.915
bubbles of total focus and create these

00:08:06.915 --> 00:08:10.100
Menlo Labs where you're
sort of, you're completely

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.576
distraction free, and
then you will find your

00:08:12.576 --> 00:08:15.493
brain will drop from the beta state

00:08:16.599 --> 00:08:19.361
into the alpha state,
where you will do your

00:08:19.361 --> 00:08:21.668
best work and get your best ideas.

00:08:21.668 --> 00:08:23.009
- But I think one of the
biggest mistakes that

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:24.937
entrepreneurs make, especially
as they grow their business,

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:26.971
or in the beginning, when
they're crippled by this

00:08:26.971 --> 00:08:29.353
thought of quality work
versus just doing it,

00:08:29.353 --> 00:08:31.962
you know, like, smart
work versus just work,

00:08:31.962 --> 00:08:34.556
is when you start judging it too much,

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:36.668
you start leaving yourself
out of opportunity for

00:08:36.668 --> 00:08:39.209
serendipity, and upside
that you can't see.

00:08:39.209 --> 00:08:40.946
There's been a lot of
things that, on paper,

00:08:40.946 --> 00:08:43.624
may look like busy work,
going to, you know,

00:08:43.624 --> 00:08:45.367
just kissing babies and shaking hands,

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.300
or just replying to everybody on Twitter,

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.087
things that not everybody
thinks is the best

00:08:49.087 --> 00:08:51.506
scalable use of my time, that
has led to enormous upside,

00:08:51.506 --> 00:08:53.536
because it wasn't obvious
when you first did it,

00:08:53.536 --> 00:08:57.065
including a video sh-,
including, 10 years ago,

00:08:57.065 --> 00:09:00.490
in a month, sitting
upstairs doing a video show,

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:03.371
where everybody's like, "We
needed you for that hour

00:09:03.371 --> 00:09:07.152
"to sell wine, or to
reorganize our operations,

00:09:07.152 --> 00:09:09.515
"why are you putting a video on YouTube,

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:12.179
"what is YouTu-," like, got it?

00:09:12.179 --> 00:09:14.286
It ended up being the
biggest, single biggest

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:16.376
decision of my career up to this point,

00:09:16.376 --> 00:09:18.275
transitioning myself into a media property

00:09:18.275 --> 00:09:20.748
and a brand from just being
an operator on this floor.

00:09:20.748 --> 00:09:23.576
Wasn't obvious, felt like
it could've been busy work,

00:09:23.576 --> 00:09:25.359
and there's been other things
that I've done like that

00:09:25.359 --> 00:09:27.692
that have been busy work,
you know, I'm trying to think

00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:29.796
of a good example, but
like, a million things that

00:09:29.796 --> 00:09:31.731
we've stopped and started
that didn't become real,

00:09:31.731 --> 00:09:33.248
it happens with us every day in our team,

00:09:33.248 --> 00:09:37.527
so I think that, I think
that you don't always know.

00:09:37.527 --> 00:09:39.819
I think you find out after the fact,

00:09:39.819 --> 00:09:42.298
and I think to have a
long 40 to 60 year old

00:09:42.298 --> 00:09:44.681
professional career that's successful,

00:09:44.681 --> 00:09:47.477
as long as you're learning
in year one, three, seven,

00:09:47.477 --> 00:09:50.681
nine, 12, patterns, start becoming more

00:09:50.681 --> 00:09:52.755
self-aware about yourself,
understanding your

00:09:52.755 --> 00:09:55.467
strengths and weaknesses,
you start having less

00:09:55.467 --> 00:09:57.717
just busy work and more high impact work,

00:09:57.717 --> 00:09:59.203
and that's what's happened in my career.

00:09:59.203 --> 00:10:00.559
- Stop multitasking.

00:10:00.559 --> 00:10:03.743
Like, I used to do so
many different things,

00:10:03.743 --> 00:10:05.746
I used to actually work in different jobs,

00:10:05.746 --> 00:10:08.241
like, do this, do that,
see, the problem about

00:10:08.241 --> 00:10:10.714
multitasking, some people can do it,

00:10:10.714 --> 00:10:12.753
but the problem is, like,
whatever you're doing,

00:10:12.753 --> 00:10:15.540
like, those different things,
they're going to suffer.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:19.213
Like, you're going to do lots of
different things really badly.

00:10:19.213 --> 00:10:21.383
So when I make videos, Infinite Waters,

00:10:21.383 --> 00:10:24.350
I'm just doing this,
like I do consultations,

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.003
I just do that.

00:10:26.003 --> 00:10:29.848
I don't do a whole lot, but
I have a lot of concentrated

00:10:29.848 --> 00:10:33.852
focus on one thing for a
sustained period of time,

00:10:33.852 --> 00:10:36.632
which is why we bloom and blossom, baby.

00:10:36.632 --> 00:10:38.512
But you don't want to do that, do you?

00:10:38.512 --> 00:10:41.475
You're like, "I want to
open up a gym, Ralph!

00:10:41.475 --> 00:10:42.902
"A restaurant, too!

00:10:42.902 --> 00:10:45.083
"Now I'm takin' my kids for a walk,

00:10:45.083 --> 00:10:48.281
"then I'm going cycling,
then I'm going to the beach,

00:10:48.281 --> 00:10:51.123
"then I'm doing this, then I'm doing that,

00:10:51.123 --> 00:10:54.055
"then I'm doing, oh my goodness gracious."

00:10:54.055 --> 00:10:56.670
You're giving yourself way too much to do,

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.649
you're making it harder than it has to be,

00:10:59.649 --> 00:11:03.145
and that's why you lose the enthusiasm

00:11:03.145 --> 00:11:05.213
for what you want to
do, so you don't end up

00:11:05.213 --> 00:11:07.783
becoming productive at all.

00:11:07.783 --> 00:11:10.816
So to become more productive,
just do one thing,

00:11:10.816 --> 00:11:14.578
and do it well, or just have fun doing it,

00:11:14.578 --> 00:11:16.171
whatever you want to do.

00:11:16.171 --> 00:11:17.835
- So I have a lot of
thoughts on productivity,

00:11:17.835 --> 00:11:19.713
I've talked a lot about
it on this channel,

00:11:19.713 --> 00:11:21.982
whether we're talkin'
about bringin' on a team

00:11:21.982 --> 00:11:23.813
or chunkin' your days and doin' different

00:11:23.813 --> 00:11:26.007
things on different days,
or not checking email,

00:11:26.007 --> 00:11:27.651
or not having a phone,
and some of the crazier

00:11:27.651 --> 00:11:30.163
things that I do to stay
productive and do as much

00:11:30.163 --> 00:11:33.257
work as I do in both my businesses now,

00:11:33.257 --> 00:11:35.218
but today I actually want
to talk about mindset.

00:11:35.218 --> 00:11:37.345
I think that mindset is super important,

00:11:37.345 --> 00:11:39.233
is extremely critical if you are thinking

00:11:39.233 --> 00:11:41.137
about how do I be
productive, 'cause you have

00:11:41.137 --> 00:11:44.037
had really productive
days, you have had days

00:11:44.037 --> 00:11:46.655
where you've watched a video or something

00:11:46.655 --> 00:11:48.329
set you off, or you had a conversation,

00:11:48.329 --> 00:11:49.850
or you read a book, or something happened,

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.183
and you were on fire
for the rest of the day,

00:11:52.183 --> 00:11:53.414
and then you wake up
the next day and that's,

00:11:53.414 --> 00:11:55.476
that feeling is gone, I mean,
you're not as productive,

00:11:55.476 --> 00:11:57.730
and even if you follow all the strategies,

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:00.779
you're applying the tactics,
you say you're going to

00:12:00.779 --> 00:12:02.356
do this this day, you
say you're not going to

00:12:02.356 --> 00:12:04.125
check your phone, you say
you're going to bring on a team,

00:12:04.125 --> 00:12:06.242
you say you're going to
apply this schedule and

00:12:06.242 --> 00:12:08.790
chunk your time, all of these ideas,

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.674
you don't always go and do them.

00:12:11.674 --> 00:12:13.190
Because you have the wrong mindset,

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:14.418
because you're not in the zone,

00:12:14.418 --> 00:12:17.277
and so, for me, what I want
to do, and what I try to do

00:12:17.277 --> 00:12:19.838
in my life, and I encourage
you to do in yours,

00:12:19.838 --> 00:12:24.005
is set up that proper
mindset every single morning.

00:12:25.237 --> 00:12:26.985
And I'm actually touching
on this in the next

00:12:26.985 --> 00:12:29.265
book that I started
writing, is creating that

00:12:29.265 --> 00:12:31.293
morning routine, creating
that morning ritual

00:12:31.293 --> 00:12:33.876
that gets you alive, every day.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.666
So instead of randomly comin' across it,

00:12:37.666 --> 00:12:39.835
maybe you watch this video
and you get inspired,

00:12:39.835 --> 00:12:41.887
that's great, but what do
you do every single day?

00:12:41.887 --> 00:12:43.831
'Cause I don't want it to happen on 4:00

00:12:43.831 --> 00:12:45.635
on a Friday afternoon, I want it to happen

00:12:45.635 --> 00:12:48.824
at 7:30 every morning, every day,

00:12:48.824 --> 00:12:51.701
so that I'm set for an
amazing productive day

00:12:51.701 --> 00:12:54.437
every single day, you can design that,

00:12:54.437 --> 00:12:56.680
instead of just reacting
to what comes into you.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.061
And so one of the things
that I do is I have

00:12:58.061 --> 00:13:01.097
a daily reminder, and I
wrote myself a quick note

00:13:01.097 --> 00:13:03.813
on what I want to feel
every day, and where I may

00:13:03.813 --> 00:13:06.818
hold myself back, and my own limitations,

00:13:06.818 --> 00:13:08.620
reminding me to play a
bigger game, and I'll read

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.449
it to you, this is what I
read to myself three times

00:13:11.449 --> 00:13:13.504
in the morning before I go off and start

00:13:13.504 --> 00:13:14.940
everything else that I have to do.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.768
Maybe you'll like it, maybe you won't.

00:13:17.768 --> 00:13:19.961
This is mine: Today, I
will push myself to be my

00:13:19.961 --> 00:13:22.985
absolute best, to believe more, care more,

00:13:22.985 --> 00:13:25.652
and try harder, I vow
not to let distractions,

00:13:25.652 --> 00:13:29.403
scheduled changes, or fear
prevent me from being great.

00:13:29.403 --> 00:13:31.553
I will make every minute
count because my time

00:13:31.553 --> 00:13:33.804
is running out, I have
the ability to change

00:13:33.804 --> 00:13:36.688
the world and it's my
responsibility to do so.

00:13:36.688 --> 00:13:38.766
Three times, I read that every morning,

00:13:38.766 --> 00:13:40.831
and then I put on my music,
and then I, you know,

00:13:40.831 --> 00:13:42.187
do a whole bunch of other
things that are part

00:13:42.187 --> 00:13:44.223
of my morning routine,
but it gets me set up,

00:13:44.223 --> 00:13:45.811
and that's what I want you to think about.

00:13:45.811 --> 00:13:47.613
Think about what you do
every single morning,

00:13:47.613 --> 00:13:49.547
'cause you don't wake
up pumped and energized,

00:13:49.547 --> 00:13:50.971
right, that alarm clock went off,

00:13:50.971 --> 00:13:52.486
you're waking up with the sun, or whatever

00:13:52.486 --> 00:13:54.254
you're wakin' up, most
people don't wake up

00:13:54.254 --> 00:13:56.330
super pumped and
energized, even if you have

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.309
a passion for what you're
doing, and you're looking

00:13:58.309 --> 00:14:00.124
forward to the day ahead, you still have

00:14:00.124 --> 00:14:02.055
to get out of bed and go do something,

00:14:02.055 --> 00:14:04.111
and I want you to figure
out ways to change

00:14:04.111 --> 00:14:07.172
your state, and to go
from that sluggish start

00:14:07.172 --> 00:14:10.143
into productivity as soon as possible,

00:14:10.143 --> 00:14:12.273
and so, thinking about
what are the emotions

00:14:12.273 --> 00:14:15.076
that you want to be
feeling, whether it's calm,

00:14:15.076 --> 00:14:17.656
whether it's excitement,
whether it's happiness,

00:14:17.656 --> 00:14:19.827
whether it's ambition,
like, what is it that

00:14:19.827 --> 00:14:21.958
you want to feel in the
morning that you want to

00:14:21.958 --> 00:14:23.398
carry through to the rest of the day,

00:14:23.398 --> 00:14:26.052
and then what do you need to do every day

00:14:26.052 --> 00:14:28.139
to feel that in the morning?

00:14:28.139 --> 00:14:30.953
Whether it's listening
to a podcast or reciting

00:14:30.953 --> 00:14:33.285
some daily reminders,
listening to some music,

00:14:33.285 --> 00:14:34.733
reading a book, watching a video,

00:14:34.733 --> 00:14:37.776
schedule that in for yourself so you set

00:14:37.776 --> 00:14:40.445
yourself up for an
amazingly productive day

00:14:40.445 --> 00:14:44.126
every single day, by design, with intent,

00:14:44.126 --> 00:14:47.626
with purpose, to create something amazing.

00:14:50.721 --> 00:14:52.642
So that's my take, I
made this video because

00:14:52.642 --> 00:14:55.620
BestyouPRO asked me to,
so if there's a topic

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.595
you'd like me to cover in the next edition

00:14:57.595 --> 00:14:59.690
of Believe Life, leave it
down in the comments below,

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:00.878
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:00.878 --> 00:15:02.861
I'd love to know what
you took from this video,

00:15:02.861 --> 00:15:04.917
who had the most meaningful message,

00:15:04.917 --> 00:15:06.428
what you're going to
immediately apply to your

00:15:06.428 --> 00:15:07.993
life or to your business,
leave it down in the

00:15:07.993 --> 00:15:10.693
comments below, I'm going
to join the discussion.

00:15:10.693 --> 00:15:13.576
If you also want to add
your own productivity hacks

00:15:13.576 --> 00:15:15.152
that we didn't cover in this video,

00:15:15.152 --> 00:15:17.275
please share it, I'm
sure it'll be extremely

00:15:17.275 --> 00:15:19.580
valuable for the entrepreneurs
watching this episode.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.590
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Jay Kahn,

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:23.876
Jay, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:15:23.876 --> 00:15:26.555
Your One Word, and posting that picture,

00:15:26.555 --> 00:15:29.375
the tribute picture to
your Instagram account,

00:15:29.375 --> 00:15:30.907
I really, really, really appreciate it.

00:15:30.907 --> 00:15:32.191
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:15:32.191 --> 00:15:34.053
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:15:34.053 --> 00:15:37.374
to believe in yourself, and
whatever your one word is,

00:15:37.374 --> 00:15:39.872
much love, have an amazing day,

00:15:39.872 --> 00:15:41.476
I'll see you soon.

00:15:41.476 --> 00:15:44.143
(air whooshing)

00:15:47.338 --> 00:15:49.232
- How do you create room?

00:15:49.232 --> 00:15:51.575
Well, one of the things
I uncovered over these

00:15:51.575 --> 00:15:53.921
35 years in developing,
really, just unfolding

00:15:53.921 --> 00:15:57.374
this methodology, is
that you get control with

00:15:57.374 --> 00:16:00.464
five stages, or five steps, and there are

00:16:00.464 --> 00:16:02.461
six horizons that we have commitments,

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:04.448
that the more clarity
you have at all those

00:16:04.448 --> 00:16:06.023
six horizons, the more
you're going to trust

00:16:06.023 --> 00:16:09.037
your judgments about what
you do, and your priorities,

00:16:09.037 --> 00:16:12.482
I couldn't get it any simpler than this.

00:16:12.482 --> 00:16:13.564
(clears throat)

00:16:13.564 --> 00:16:17.828
Those five steps, pretty simple, actually.

00:16:17.828 --> 00:16:22.077
I didn't make these up, I recognized them.

00:16:22.077 --> 00:16:23.403
All right, this is how
you get your kitchen

00:16:23.403 --> 00:16:25.587
under control, you get
your company under control,

00:16:25.587 --> 00:16:29.245
and you get your
consciousness under control.

00:16:29.245 --> 00:16:31.629
Doesn't happen by itself.

00:16:31.629 --> 00:16:34.263
Any of you ever had your
kitchen out of control?

00:16:34.263 --> 00:16:35.515
Anybody ever come home and it looked like

00:16:35.515 --> 00:16:38.042
your kitchen had been attacked?

00:16:38.042 --> 00:16:40.270
Oh my God, and you got friends
coming over in an hour,

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:42.330
and you're supposed to cook spaghetti.

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.534
Oh no, what's the first thing you did

00:16:44.534 --> 00:16:47.617
besides drink or call out for dinner?

00:16:48.775 --> 00:16:52.869
First thing you did was you
looked at what was not right.

00:16:52.869 --> 00:16:55.869
You identified something
that's not on cruise control

00:16:55.869 --> 00:16:58.345
in that environment or echo system.

00:16:58.345 --> 00:17:02.512
That's, you noticed what
had your attention, right?

00:17:04.463 --> 00:17:05.967
Then what did you do?

00:17:05.967 --> 00:17:07.932
You decided, "What does that stuff mean?

00:17:07.932 --> 00:17:10.361
"Ooh, that's a dirty
dish, oh, that's a spice,

00:17:10.361 --> 00:17:12.996
"oh, that's a clean dish,
oh, that's good food,

00:17:12.996 --> 00:17:14.842
"oh, that's bad food."

00:17:14.842 --> 00:17:17.333
You clarified exactly
what those things are that

00:17:17.333 --> 00:17:19.311
are not on cruise control.

00:17:19.311 --> 00:17:20.479
Then what did you do?

00:17:20.479 --> 00:17:22.356
You organized them where they go.

00:17:22.356 --> 00:17:23.901
You put dirty dishes in the dishwasher,

00:17:23.901 --> 00:17:27.484
you put good food in
the fridge, et cetera.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.283
Once you determined what it means,

00:17:30.283 --> 00:17:33.156
you parked it where it should
go, then what did you do?

00:17:33.156 --> 00:17:35.185
You looked at the whole
scene, you stepped back,

00:17:35.185 --> 00:17:38.048
reflected on the whole gestalt.

00:17:38.048 --> 00:17:41.761
Looked at the time,
pulled out maybe a recipe,

00:17:41.761 --> 00:17:46.353
and then you pulled out
butter and started to melt it.

00:17:46.353 --> 00:17:48.727
Now, that's how you get
your kitchen under control,

00:17:48.727 --> 00:17:49.860
but that's also how you get your

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.792
consciousness under control.

00:17:51.792 --> 00:17:53.466
If I were to sit down
with you and you said,

00:17:53.466 --> 00:17:56.946
"God, things are feeling,
oh, overwhelming!"

00:17:56.946 --> 00:17:59.317
First thing I'm going to
do, capture everything

00:17:59.317 --> 00:18:01.370
that has your attention.

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:03.395
That takes one to six hours, by the way,

00:18:03.395 --> 00:18:06.780
for most people, just to
identify what's got your mind.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.292
(clears throat)

00:18:08.292 --> 00:18:09.686
Then we're going to go
through each one of them

00:18:09.686 --> 00:18:11.554
and decide what's the
very next action step

00:18:11.554 --> 00:18:14.048
required for that thing,
and what's the final

00:18:14.048 --> 00:18:16.940
outcome that you're
committed to finish about it?

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:18.938
The clarification step.

00:18:18.938 --> 00:18:21.251
Then we're going to work
with you to customize,

00:18:21.251 --> 00:18:24.094
how do I keep track of
the calls I need to make,

00:18:24.094 --> 00:18:26.742
the errands I need to run,
the stuff I need to talk

00:18:26.742 --> 00:18:29.978
to my life partner about,
where do I put those things

00:18:29.978 --> 00:18:32.896
so that my head is not trying to do it?

00:18:32.896 --> 00:18:36.313
By the way, your head is a crappy office.

00:18:37.171 --> 00:18:39.072
Anything you're trying to
manage just in your head,

00:18:39.072 --> 00:18:41.263
believe me, you will
over or underreact to it

00:18:41.263 --> 00:18:42.846
if it's just there.

00:18:43.705 --> 00:18:45.064
And that's one of the things
that's happened in the last

00:18:45.064 --> 00:18:46.770
15 years since I wrote the first edition

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.244
of Getting Things Done,
is how much the cognitive

00:18:49.244 --> 00:18:51.589
scientists have now
validated the fact that

00:18:51.589 --> 00:18:54.610
your head is for having ideas,
but not for holding 'em.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:56.263
As a matter of fact, if you're
trying to hang on to more

00:18:56.263 --> 00:19:00.430
than four meaningful things
in your head, you're toast.

00:19:01.304 --> 00:19:03.962
So building the external brain
requires that you capture

00:19:03.962 --> 00:19:05.431
the stuff that has your attention,

00:19:05.431 --> 00:19:08.702
clarify actions and outcomes,
organize the results

00:19:08.702 --> 00:19:11.444
of that in some external
trusted system so that you can

00:19:11.444 --> 00:19:13.506
then step back and reflect on all of that,

00:19:13.506 --> 00:19:17.028
and then decide to have a
beer instead of any of it.

00:19:17.028 --> 00:19:19.605
But that's a high performance beer.

00:19:19.605 --> 00:19:21.352
I'm not voting for
alcohol as a way of life,

00:19:21.352 --> 00:19:24.710
I'm just self-disclosing,
I love good beer.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:29.529
Poland's a great place
for that, I've discovered.

00:19:29.529 --> 00:19:32.340
So that's, those five steps, by the way,

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.217
are how we basically work with people,

00:19:35.217 --> 00:19:37.506
what we train people
about, and most people,

00:19:37.506 --> 00:19:40.177
even though these are simple,
have a huge improvement

00:19:40.177 --> 00:19:42.541
opportunity in terms
of making sure that you

00:19:42.541 --> 00:19:46.028
externalize your commitments
at multiple levels,

00:19:46.028 --> 00:19:49.072
so that you can see 'em from
where you need to see 'em.

00:19:49.072 --> 00:19:50.563
- So what do you think holds people back?

00:19:50.563 --> 00:19:52.140
They, they, they know
what they want to do,

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.441
but they just can't keep
the habit, they do it once,

00:19:54.441 --> 00:19:56.521
and then they fall off the
train, and then next month

00:19:56.521 --> 00:19:58.023
they do it again, and
they fall off the train,

00:19:58.023 --> 00:20:00.334
how do you make that consistent?

00:20:00.334 --> 00:20:03.067
- Well, you just keep, you
just keep working at it

00:20:03.067 --> 00:20:06.352
and working at it, the
best way of all is to plan

00:20:06.352 --> 00:20:08.564
it in advance; instead of sitting down,

00:20:08.564 --> 00:20:10.724
never start work without
a list, and never start

00:20:10.724 --> 00:20:13.198
work without prioritizing your list.

00:20:13.198 --> 00:20:15.475
People sit down and they
say, "Well, before I make

00:20:15.475 --> 00:20:18.833
"a list, I'll check a little
email, check a little spam,

00:20:18.833 --> 00:20:20.934
"send something to my
friend, see what's going

00:20:20.934 --> 00:20:23.456
"on YouTube," and so on and so forth,

00:20:23.456 --> 00:20:25.713
and then they look up and it's 10:00,

00:20:25.713 --> 00:20:27.677
and they haven't done anything of value.

00:20:27.677 --> 00:20:29.635
So what you do is you have to s-,

00:20:29.635 --> 00:20:32.552
is say, you know, "Well," you know,

00:20:33.798 --> 00:20:37.148
what do they say, if you start it right,

00:20:37.148 --> 00:20:40.277
it'll work out right, so start it as,

00:20:40.277 --> 00:20:42.795
on your most important task, and do that

00:20:42.795 --> 00:20:45.278
until it becomes a habit,
and here's an interesting

00:20:45.278 --> 00:20:48.842
thing about checking email is when you

00:20:48.842 --> 00:20:52.620
check email, it triggers
the release of dopamine

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:55.572
in your brain, dopamine is a drug,

00:20:55.572 --> 00:20:57.264
and by the way, there's all kinds of books

00:20:57.264 --> 00:20:59.317
and articles and
scientific studies on this,

00:20:59.317 --> 00:21:02.565
and dopamine is the same drug in cocaine,

00:21:02.565 --> 00:21:04.769
and what it does is it's
triggered every time

00:21:04.769 --> 00:21:08.102
you have a shiny object or something new

00:21:09.032 --> 00:21:11.495
or different or interesting or sparkly.

00:21:11.495 --> 00:21:13.915
If a telephone rings next
to you, you go "Bing!"

00:21:13.915 --> 00:21:16.172
your brain releases
endorphins, and you can't

00:21:16.172 --> 00:21:17.824
hardly wait to see what it is.

00:21:17.824 --> 00:21:19.649
So you're talking to
somebody, and you could be

00:21:19.649 --> 00:21:21.494
talking to somebody about
something very serious,

00:21:21.494 --> 00:21:23.837
and your telephone goes off, "Bing!"

00:21:23.837 --> 00:21:25.966
Your whole mind goes blank.

00:21:25.966 --> 00:21:28.152
The middle of a conversation stops,

00:21:28.152 --> 00:21:29.614
so you grab for you phone, you pick it up,

00:21:29.614 --> 00:21:32.025
you look at it, you check
it out and everything else,

00:21:32.025 --> 00:21:34.560
and then you come back, if
you're working on a task,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.402
it takes an average of 17
minutes to get back to the task,

00:21:37.402 --> 00:21:41.585
and then there's another
distraction, so another 17 minutes.

00:21:41.585 --> 00:21:44.266
Average adult is checking
their email between,

00:21:44.266 --> 00:21:49.084
and their phone, between
95 and 145 times a day.

00:21:49.084 --> 00:21:51.037
- That's crazy.

00:21:51.037 --> 00:21:54.571
- Which means they are like
Attention Deficit Disorder dogs,

00:21:54.571 --> 00:21:56.515
they're just literally running
around, back and forth,

00:21:56.515 --> 00:21:58.446
the whole day, at the end of
the day, they're exhausted.

00:21:58.446 --> 00:22:01.556
By the way, USA Today
and Harvard have proved

00:22:01.556 --> 00:22:05.389
that you lose one IQ
point for every hour that

00:22:06.223 --> 00:22:09.238
you leave your email on,
at the end of the day,

00:22:09.238 --> 00:22:11.965
you've lost 10 IQ points,
you actually become

00:22:11.965 --> 00:22:14.298
stupider throughout the day, dumber,

00:22:14.298 --> 00:22:16.934
and here's a, I ask this of my audiences,

00:22:16.934 --> 00:22:19.422
I say, "Have you ever finished a day where

00:22:19.422 --> 00:22:20.603
"you don't think you've
accomplished anything,

00:22:20.603 --> 00:22:23.650
"you get on email,
responding to bings and beeps

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:25.908
"and rings and so on all day,
and at the end of the day,

00:22:25.908 --> 00:22:29.781
"you go home and honey says,
'What do you want for dinner,'

00:22:29.781 --> 00:22:32.449
"and you say, 'I don't know,
I don't care, anything.

00:22:32.449 --> 00:22:33.713
"'I just want a drink.'"

00:22:33.713 --> 00:22:36.943
Because you're so exhausted,
you cannot even decide

00:22:36.943 --> 00:22:39.230
(audio cuts out) sit there and basically

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:41.176
like you've just been, you know,

00:22:41.176 --> 00:22:42.731
punched in the side of the head,

00:22:42.731 --> 00:22:45.257
because your brain, your brain is dead,

00:22:45.257 --> 00:22:48.191
and you lose 10 IQ
points, you get stupider

00:22:48.191 --> 00:22:50.450
throughout the day,
you've got to turn that

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:53.798
darn thing off if you
want to remain sharp.

00:22:53.798 --> 00:22:55.812
(audio cuts out) develop a habit, you say,

00:22:55.812 --> 00:22:58.138
"I'm going to develop a
habit, and I'm going to give

00:22:58.138 --> 00:23:00.438
"myself my reward, and my
reward will be to check

00:23:00.438 --> 00:23:03.767
"my email after I've
finished my first task."

00:23:03.767 --> 00:23:07.808
So, all of success in
life is task completion.

00:23:07.808 --> 00:23:11.475
Let me repeat that, I just
had a long conversation

00:23:11.475 --> 00:23:13.971
with an entrepreneur, he
almost fell of his chair.

00:23:13.971 --> 00:23:15.948
I was coaching him, and he said he'd

00:23:15.948 --> 00:23:17.796
never heard that before,
and then I spoke to,

00:23:17.796 --> 00:23:20.254
with this group I was
telling you in Japan,

00:23:20.254 --> 00:23:21.831
I told them the same thing,

00:23:21.831 --> 00:23:23.548
and they were just flabbergasted.

00:23:23.548 --> 00:23:26.700
Is, the key to success in
life is task completion.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:29.411
If you don't complete
tasks, you have no future,

00:23:29.411 --> 00:23:32.481
and so, if task completion is the key,

00:23:32.481 --> 00:23:34.173
then what you do is you
complete more and more

00:23:34.173 --> 00:23:36.861
important tasks, and
if you start every day

00:23:36.861 --> 00:23:40.328
by completing the most important task,

00:23:40.328 --> 00:23:43.201
your brain actually releases endorphins.

00:23:43.201 --> 00:23:45.371
Now, endorphins is another chemical,

00:23:45.371 --> 00:23:47.301
and it's only released
when you do something

00:23:47.301 --> 00:23:50.143
that is life enhancing,
and what that means

00:23:50.143 --> 00:23:52.582
is when you do something
that is good for you,

00:23:52.582 --> 00:23:54.624
that enhances your
life, that furthers you,

00:23:54.624 --> 00:23:57.604
it makes you happy, it releases energy,

00:23:57.604 --> 00:24:01.317
it raises your self-esteem, I sort of say,

00:24:01.317 --> 00:24:04.006
"If you want to be a
winner, how do be a winner,

00:24:04.006 --> 00:24:06.988
"well, you win, and winning
means you cross a finish line."

00:24:06.988 --> 00:24:09.147
So if you start and complete a task,

00:24:09.147 --> 00:24:11.615
you're actually winning,
and you get a feeling

00:24:11.615 --> 00:24:14.903
of being a winner, which
gives you a suffusion

00:24:14.903 --> 00:24:18.264
of energy, and inspiration, and it drives

00:24:18.264 --> 00:24:20.257
you and motivates you to want to get that

00:24:20.257 --> 00:24:22.636
feeling again, so how do
you get that feeling again?

00:24:22.636 --> 00:24:24.150
You start to complete another task.

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:26.790
So I've developed a
habit, and people who are

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:28.801
successful developed a habit of starting

00:24:28.801 --> 00:24:31.325
and completing tasks, so my wife comes in

00:24:31.325 --> 00:24:33.120
and says, "Dinner's in five minutes,"

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.275
so I look at my list and I say,

00:24:34.275 --> 00:24:36.305
"Okay, there's a five minute task,"

00:24:36.305 --> 00:24:39.038
and I get ready to start and
complete a five minute task.

00:24:39.038 --> 00:24:40.691
And each time you complete a task,

00:24:40.691 --> 00:24:42.620
you get a burst of endorphin,
the bigger the task,

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:44.608
the more endorphin, the more endorphin,

00:24:44.608 --> 00:24:47.232
the more quickly you
become addicted to starting

00:24:47.232 --> 00:24:49.954
and completing important
tasks, and every single

00:24:49.954 --> 00:24:52.955
successful person has a
reputation for starting

00:24:52.955 --> 00:24:54.623
and completing important tasks.

00:24:54.623 --> 00:24:56.693
You want the job done,
give it to him or her.

00:24:56.693 --> 00:24:58.688
You want the product served on time,

00:24:58.688 --> 00:25:00.242
you want the product
completed, you want the

00:25:00.242 --> 00:25:02.101
product manufactured, shipped, delivered,

00:25:02.101 --> 00:25:04.401
give it to him, give it to
her, give it to that company.

00:25:04.401 --> 00:25:06.010
'Cause they always deliver.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.800
That's the best reputation you can

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.014
have if you want to get rich.

